+EchoForge uses a clean-room signal-chain proxy rather than a proprietary sensor surrogate. The design rule is simple: use radar best practices to make the synthetic evidence physically legible, but do not imply measured-platform truth. The simulation begins with public-proxy geometry and kinematics, then applies branch-specific radar resolution assumptions, phase windows, clutter/noise/RFI stressors, and receiver impairments. The output is not an operational radar stream. It is a reproducible synthetic lane from which detector-view features can be audited.
+
+\subsection{Signal-Simulation Best Practices and EchoForge Choices}
+The simulation follows five radar-review principles.
+\begin{enumerate}
+  \item \emph{Separate geometry from truth.} Airframe geometry, aspect, range, and phase state are public-proxy priors, not measured signatures.
+  \item \emph{Use derived resolution quantities.} Bandwidth, CPI, PRF/CRF, range bins, and carrier frequency determine the displayed $\Delta R$, $\Delta f_D$, and $\Delta v$ values.
+  \item \emph{Stress the detector, not the claim boundary.} Weibull/K-like clutter, weather/sea/terrain glint, multipath ghosts, RFI bursts, AGC, clock drift, quantization, dropped CPI, and Doppler folding create failure pressure without becoming site-measured truth.
+  \item \emph{Keep detector views narrower than generator state.} Model-facing matrices receive score summaries and cue features, not labels, group identifiers, split keys, or restricted generator metadata.
+  \item \emph{Evaluate by group.} Scenario groups, not phase rows, are the bootstrap unit because one group creates multiple phase records.
+\end{enumerate}
+
