+\subsection{Why Sensor Fusion Is the Accepted Comparator}
+Low-altitude UAS detection is rarely a single-detector problem. Radar branches see range-Doppler and track structure; acoustic cues see cadence and cross-node agreement; passive-RF cues see absence, contamination, and provenance. The accepted fusion baseline is therefore the strongest non-EI comparator because it combines branch evidence while retaining a human-designed threshold and calibration policy. EI is judged against this fusion lane, not against a deliberately weak single sensor.
+
