+\subsection{Decomposition, Evolution, and Dynamic Objectives}
+EI is implemented as an auditable algorithm-design workflow rather than a black-box model. It decomposes the problem into candidate score families, evolves and ranks sparse nonnegative combinations on train/CV rows, fits a monotone odds calibrator, writes a selection lock, and then scores holdout once. This is a dynamic algorithm-development pattern: the objective can change from AP to low-FPR recall, calibration-aware F1, group-level recall, hard-negative burden, or phase-minimum recall, but the holdout isolation rule does not change.
+
+\begin{table*}[t]
+  \caption{EI Objective Families Available for Future Runs}
+  \label{tab:eiobjectives}
+  \centering
+  \small
+  \begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.20\textwidth}p{0.35\textwidth}p{0.33\textwidth}@{}}
+    \toprule
+    Objective & What it rewards & Why a reviewer might care \\
+    \midrule
+    LCB95 Recall@$\leq$1\%FPR & conservative low-FPR detection under group-block uncertainty & Current headline KPI \\
+    Calibration-aware F1 & selected-threshold precision/recall with probability reliability & Prevents high-AP but poorly calibrated controls from becoming the claim \\
+    Hard-negative burden & few false alarms on birds, RC aircraft, weather, clutter, multipath, and RFI & Makes failure modes operationally legible without claiming field rates \\
+    Phase-minimum recall & worst-phase low-FPR recall across take-up, climb, cruise & Prevents cruise-only success from hiding take-up weakness \\
+    Cost-weighted utility & application-specific penalty matrix over miss, false alarm, and abstain & Future policy-level objective, not used as the current claim \\
+    \bottomrule
+  \end{tabular}
+\end{table*}
